<article class="env-lemma" id="0G3I">
  <p><a class="environment-identifier" href="/tag/0G3I">Lemma <span data-tag="0G3I">15.95.11</span>.</a> Let $A$ be a ring derived complete with respect to an ideal $I$. Set $J = \bigcap I^ n$. If $I$ can be generated by $r$ elements then $J^ N = 0$ where $N = 2^ r$. </p>
</article><article class="env-proof">
    <p>
      <strong>Proof.</strong>
      When $r = 1$ this is Lemma <a href="/tag/0G3G" data-tag="0G3G">15.95.9</a>. Say $I = (f_1, \ldots , f_ r)$ with $r > 1$. By Lemma <a href="/tag/091U" data-tag="091U">15.93.6</a> the ring $A_ t = A/f_ r^ tA$ is derived complete with respect to $I$ and hence a fortiori derived complete with respect to $I_ t = (f_1, \ldots , f_{r - 1})A_ t$. Observe that $A \to A_ t$ sends $J$ into $J_ t = \bigcap I_ t^ n$. By induction $J_ t^{N/2} = 0$ with $N = 2^ r$. The ideal $\bigcap \mathop{\mathrm{Ker}}(A \to A_ t) = \bigcap f_ r^ t A$ has square zero by the case $r = 1$. This finishes the proof. 
      <span class="qed">$\square$</span>
    </p>
</article>